FUSE-BPF backing directory regression test
========================================

Build against exported kernel headers and libc headers providing
linux/fscrypt.h. From the kernel root, for example:

    make ARCH=arm64 headers_install INSTALL_HDR_PATH=/tmp/fuse-headers
    make -C tools/testing/selftests/filesystems/fuse \
        CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu- LDFLAGS=-static \
        KHDR_INCLUDES=-I/tmp/fuse-headers/include

Run as root on a disposable F2FS filesystem with encryption and
casefold:

    ./backing-casefold /mnt/test-f2fs

Each variant uses its own scratch directory on that filesystem and a
FUSE mountpoint under /tmp. Both are removed afterward. No existing
files are removed and no supplied directory flags are changed. The
backing-only BPF filter has two instructions. Its daemon serves only
INIT, so creation cannot silently fall back to userspace.

Enable FUSE_FS, FUSE_BPF, BPF_SYSCALL, F2FS_FS, UNICODE, FS_ENCRYPTION
and their dependencies. This tree also needs FS_ENCRYPTION_INLINE_CRYPT
and BLK_INLINE_ENCRYPTION for fscrypt v2 key management ioctls. The
Exynos9810 configs enable these options. Crypto hardware is not needed.
Root needs permission to load BPF, mount FUSE, and manage fscrypt keys.

The variants are ordinary, casefolded, and encrypted casefolded
parents. They cover nested mkdir, OBB staging and finalization, file
creation and reopen, negative lookups, lower creation, FIFO, symlink,
hardlink, rename, removal, Unicode and mixed-case lookup and mutation,
and concurrent lower/upper creation of equivalent names.

The encryption key is a public fixed test value. It remains installed
until the test filesystem unmounts. Use a disposable filesystem rather
than an existing userdata tree. This manual test is built and installed
by kselftest; the automatic runner cannot supply the test filesystem.
Missing arguments or a non-root caller returns kselftest skip (4).

Output uses TAP. Failed operations or missing required features fail
the test. A process alarm bounds runtime; a VM runner should also use
a host timeout for kernel hangs or uninterruptible waits.
